WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.360
In order to speak well,
you have to speak a lot.

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:07.119
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here,
and today I want to talk about

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:09.254
talking your way to fluency.

00:00:09.615 --> 00:00:12.105
Remember, if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe.

00:00:12.105 --> 00:00:13.905
Click on the bell for notifications.

00:00:13.965 --> 00:00:16.185
If you follow me on a podcast
service, leave a comment.

00:00:16.185 --> 00:00:17.055
I do appreciate it.

00:00:17.535 --> 00:00:22.305
So we often, you know, hear people say
that they can talk their way to fluency.

00:00:23.055 --> 00:00:27.165
People have the impression that speaking
the language is the key to becoming

00:00:27.165 --> 00:00:30.975
fluent in the language, the key to
learning the language, we have to use it.

00:00:31.095 --> 00:00:37.110
In Japan, they have these, you know,
groups, English conversation groups

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:41.310
where people gather around and speak in
English and, and so I think the idea that

00:00:41.315 --> 00:00:46.380
you're gonna speak your way to progress
in the language is, is quite popular.

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:50.205
And I'm not saying that that's,
you know, counterproductive.

00:00:50.685 --> 00:00:54.315
But I would like to suggest some
ways to make it more effective.

00:00:54.435 --> 00:00:58.995
So I think there are two possible
scenarios, or two polls here.

00:00:59.025 --> 00:01:02.475
One is that you're in a place
where the language is spoken.

00:01:02.475 --> 00:01:03.945
You're surrounded by the language.

00:01:04.065 --> 00:01:09.525
You have lots of opportunity to use the
language, and I think in that situation,

00:01:10.155 --> 00:01:12.555
speaking your way to fluency can work.

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:17.190
Remember, in order to speak
well, you have to speak a lot.

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:22.229
So the only question is when are
you able to, or when should you

00:01:22.259 --> 00:01:26.190
speak more and more and more in
order to get better at speaking?

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:29.970
So, uh, when I was in Japan,
wherever I had an opportunity, I

00:01:29.975 --> 00:01:34.600
was in the country, I used whatever
little bit of Japanese that I had.

00:01:35.230 --> 00:01:37.455
Although I maintained my input activities.

00:01:37.934 --> 00:01:43.095
So if you're surrounded by friends, you're
in the country, you've met people, maybe

00:01:43.095 --> 00:01:48.285
you have a common interest, you're playing
tennis or you're into some other activity.

00:01:48.554 --> 00:01:52.994
So you're with people that you know,
uh, it's a comfortable environment.

00:01:53.085 --> 00:01:56.054
It becomes increasingly
a familiar environment.

00:01:56.445 --> 00:02:02.065
Uh, you have common interests, so
actually your input, excuse me, your

00:02:02.065 --> 00:02:07.545
output, your speaking is triggering
input for you because you are able

00:02:07.545 --> 00:02:12.555
to participate initially just a
little bit, eventually more and more.

00:02:12.765 --> 00:02:15.825
And when you talk about
something, something more or

00:02:15.825 --> 00:02:17.775
less familiar to you comes back.

00:02:17.895 --> 00:02:21.405
I always found, even when I'm
quite good in a language, an

00:02:21.405 --> 00:02:27.300
unfamiliar, you know, situation,
it's much more difficult for me.

00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:31.350
Whereas if it's a familiar
situation, I can react, I can almost

00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:33.270
predict what's going to be said.

00:02:33.330 --> 00:02:36.810
And I think if you're in a country
where the language is spoken and

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:41.580
you're with people a lot, and familiar
people and subjects that, uh, you are

00:02:41.585 --> 00:02:48.885
comfortable talking about, uh, and you
get more and more input back at you,

00:02:49.185 --> 00:02:53.355
then you are learning and improving
in a situation, and you're learning

00:02:53.355 --> 00:02:57.524
primarily because of the input you're
getting back, and also to some extent,

00:02:57.674 --> 00:03:01.935
because you're outputting some of the
same words and phrases and concepts.

00:03:01.935 --> 00:03:03.855
So that's a, an ideal scenario.

00:03:03.915 --> 00:03:08.445
Takes a while to get traction, but
slowly, slowly, slowly by being with

00:03:08.445 --> 00:03:11.280
people, friends of yours, you do improve.

00:03:11.820 --> 00:03:13.470
But let's look at the other situation.

00:03:13.470 --> 00:03:17.460
Let's say that as is the case with
me right now and many language

00:03:17.465 --> 00:03:20.850
learners, you're in a place
where the language is not spoken.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:27.240
So you have to now create opportunities
to speak typically with an online

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:33.060
language partner or with a tutor,
or perhaps you can find people in

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:37.200
your, you know, wherever you live,
that speak the target language.

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:39.105
But that's more difficult to organize.

00:03:39.535 --> 00:03:45.810
So inevitably in the second scenario,
you are speaking less often, so

00:03:45.870 --> 00:03:49.560
you are getting less, you know,
input material coming back at you.

00:03:49.710 --> 00:03:50.550
What can you do?

00:03:50.790 --> 00:03:54.210
Well, here again, I think
you have to organize as much

00:03:54.270 --> 00:03:56.670
input material as possible.

00:03:57.180 --> 00:04:02.790
And as is the case when you are in a,
you know, situation where you can speak

00:04:02.790 --> 00:04:09.464
a lot, you wanna try to, you know, input
or find input or surround yourself with

00:04:09.464 --> 00:04:14.954
input material that is familiar, that
is comfortable for you, and therefore

00:04:14.954 --> 00:04:17.294
depending on your level in the language.

00:04:17.774 --> 00:04:22.215
You can go to the mini stories,
particularly if, uh, you have learned

00:04:22.545 --> 00:04:28.065
another language, say at LingQ, and so the
mini stories are already familiar to you.

00:04:28.365 --> 00:04:32.534
Then you can learn a second language
with these mini stories, but even if

00:04:32.534 --> 00:04:36.465
it's your first language that you're
learning, because the mini stories,

00:04:37.229 --> 00:04:40.219
the same content basically repeats.

00:04:40.309 --> 00:04:42.559
So familiar is good.

00:04:42.590 --> 00:04:46.489
The more familiar the content
is, the better you're learning.

00:04:46.760 --> 00:04:51.049
And then as you progress in the
language, you'll be, I think you

00:04:51.049 --> 00:04:56.450
want to bring in content to listen
to that is of interest to you.

00:04:56.985 --> 00:04:58.245
maybe you're familiar with it.

00:04:58.245 --> 00:05:02.805
It might be sports, it might be some
other, even, for example, a series

00:05:02.805 --> 00:05:06.975
on Netflix where the same people,
the same family meets, you know,

00:05:06.980 --> 00:05:11.175
every episode familiar is good.

00:05:11.625 --> 00:05:16.185
Also, it now gives you something
to talk to your tutor about.

00:05:16.515 --> 00:05:25.125
I have begun with, uh, say Persiabn
doing the mini stories and then having

00:05:25.125 --> 00:05:29.505
that tutor ask me those very same
questions that show up in the mini story.

00:05:30.044 --> 00:05:34.034
Uh, I've even read with her reading
the mini story because Persian reading,

00:05:34.034 --> 00:05:35.445
Persian for me was very difficult.

00:05:35.745 --> 00:05:39.164
So you can use the mini stories
as again, familiar content.

00:05:39.554 --> 00:05:42.354
Later on, you can work with your tutor.

00:05:42.955 --> 00:05:46.795
So that your online conversations can
deal with something that is of interest

00:05:46.795 --> 00:05:50.395
to you, which could be sports, which
could be cooking, which could be whatever.

00:05:50.455 --> 00:05:55.015
And the tutor might even help you find
content that's in this area of interest.

00:05:55.135 --> 00:05:59.335
So again, you're dealing with material
that's familiar to you, that's gonna

00:05:59.340 --> 00:06:03.535
make it easier to talk about those
to speak, and it's gonna make it

00:06:03.535 --> 00:06:08.935
easier to use that input material
to power up your ability to speak.

00:06:09.235 --> 00:06:14.655
So to summarize, I think whether
we can speak our way to fluency

00:06:14.655 --> 00:06:17.055
or not depends on where we are.

00:06:17.385 --> 00:06:21.344
But in either case, whether we are
in a place where the language is

00:06:21.344 --> 00:06:25.005
spoken or whether we are not in a
place where the language is spoken,

00:06:25.245 --> 00:06:26.985
we are gonna need a lot of input.

00:06:27.465 --> 00:06:32.305
And the more familiar that input is, the
more we're dealing with things that you

00:06:32.310 --> 00:06:36.735
like, the better off you're gonna do in
the better you're gonna do in both cases.

00:06:36.885 --> 00:06:39.945
And in both cases, you'll be
more comfortable talking about

00:06:39.945 --> 00:06:41.025
things that you're familiar.

00:06:41.370 --> 00:06:47.159
So just a thought on, uh, this issue of,
of, uh, speaking your way to fluency.

00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:51.480
So I hope that was helpful and I
have spoken on this subject before,

00:06:51.870 --> 00:06:55.080
and I'll leave you with a couple
of videos that talk about the

00:06:55.080 --> 00:06:57.600
relationship between input and output.

00:06:57.840 --> 00:06:58.770
Thank you for listening.

00:06:58.920 --> 00:06:59.370
Bye for now.

